    "case_id": "753",
    "case_summary": "A 4-year-old female was initially referred to the Genetics department at 20 days of age. At 21 months, she experienced weekly recurrent episodes of fixed gaze, absence-like loss of contact, and myoclonic-like movements of upper and lower arms lasting from a few seconds to 15 minutes, with frequent falls. Development history showed gross motor development was adequate, but fine motor, language, and sociability skills were at the level of 16 months. Family history includes mother with moderate intellectual disability and childhood-onset epilepsy, maternal grandmother with late-onset epilepsy, and maternal aunt with mild intellectual impairment.\n\nPhysical examination revealed normal tonus and reflexes, mild psychomotor delay, and broad-based gait. Notable features included: hypotonic face, high frontal hairline, thin long eyebrows with lateral extension and medial flare, marked epicanthus inversus, upslanting palpebral fissures, round nostrils, full cheeks, protruding lower lip, apparently low-set and posteriorly rotated ears with up-lifted ear lobes, simple descending helix, protruding descending anthelix, increased intermamillary distance, mild pectus excavatum, atypical hemangiomas over the chest left and on the inside of left upper arm, 5th-finger clinodactyly, and a broad 1st toe. Growth parameters: weight 12,600g (80th centile), length 89cm (90th centile), and OFC 46.6cm (25th centile, relative microcephaly).\n\n- Laboratory Tests: All routine hematological, biochemical, and thyroid tests were normal.\n- Imaging Studies:\n  * Brain MRI showed mild frontal colpocephaly\n- Other Tests:\n  * Sleep-deprived electroencephalogram (EEG) was normal\n  * Ophthalmological examination revealed mild hyperopia, intermittent strabismus and nystagmus of congenital origin\n  * Otolaryngological and cardiological tests were normal\n- Genetic Tests:\n  * Cytogenetic analysis revealed 46,XX,inv(16)(q22.3q24.1)mat\n  * Microarray analysis showed no cryptic microdeletions or microduplications\n  * Clinical Exome Sequencing detected a pathogenic nonsense variant in the CHD2 gene [NM_001271.4:c.5035C>T p.(Arg1679Ter)]",
